## Technical Notes

### Session Management

- Spring Security uses session cookies (JSESSIONID)
- Sessions are stored in-memory (default)
- For production, consider using Redis or database session store

### Security Considerations

- OAuth2 client credentials are stored in environment variables
- Never commit [.env](file:///home/pran/anotherDrive/javaCodes/CPA/.env) file to version control
- HTTPS required for production OAuth2 flows
- GitHub callback URL must match exactly

### Backward Compatibility

- HTTP Basic Auth still works for API access
- Useful for programmatic API clients
- Can be deprecated gradually

### Database Schema

The `oauth2_users` table is separate from the in-memory users used by Basic Auth. This allows:
- Clean separation of concerns
- Easy migration path
- Ability to maintain both auth methods

---

